+# Export acceptance and restart recovery hunt
+
+Checked 2026-10-24 at baseline `c8ec161dbfbfdac209ecc6fdeb00c2094eb5ff51`
+using Python 3.9.6. Product code, governing records, and existing evidence were
+preserved. This report and `tests/hunt_witness.py` are the only additions.
+
+## Scope and authority
+
+The behavioral authority is `app/export-contract.md`: local SQLite, committed
+transactions survive process termination, one worker, sequential requests,
+eventual cessation of interruptions and repeated calls to work after recovery.
+Storage loss and corruption are excluded by that contract. Prioritized harms
+were abandoned accepted jobs, false acceptance, and incorrect repeated effects.
+The stopping boundary was acceptance and its direct work/restart paths, checked
+at pending, running, and completed states and during result transaction failure.
+
+Read `app/export_queue.py`, its contract, all root records, and the export and
+single-writer claim evidence. Searched app, tests, specs, README, and AGENTS for
+queue consumers and record references; no other Queue consumer or deployment
+configuration was found. The note handler/exporter and Store are separate from
+this queue. The local-only gate remains respected; unrelated source metadata
+requirements and note payload behavior are outside this hunt.
+
+This is a hunt informed by historical evidence, not a fresh independent claim
+verification. No claim statement, evidence, or verdict was changed.
+
+## Demonstrated unresolved defect
+
+**Accepted work is permanently ineligible after its running state commits.**
+`Queue.submit` commits pending work before returning acceptance
+(`app/export_queue.py:21–25`). `work` commits running separately from the result
+transaction (`:33–39`). Interruption between those commits leaves running with
+no result. Startup calls `recover`, which does nothing (`:15–19`), and work only
+selects pending rows (`:28–30`). Consequently, even after all faults stop and
+work is called repeatedly, this job cannot be selected. The durable missing
+eligibility, rather than a finite retry count alone, establishes abandonment.
+
+This is the existing falsification in
+`specs/CLAIM-export-recovery/verification.md`, reproduced by
+`python3 tests/known_loss.py` (exit 1, “accepted work abandoned after restart”).
+It remains unresolved.
+
+The new witness strengthens and extends the triggering coverage:
+
+- A child commits acceptance and running, then calls `os._exit(23)` at the
+  documented after-claim boundary. A new process connection sees the job still
+  running, three work calls produce no result, and work remains ineligible.
+- A temporary SQLite trigger aborts the done update after result insertion.
+  The result transaction rolls back, correctly removing its partial result,
+  but the earlier running commit survives. Removing the trigger and retrying,
+  both live and after reopening, still produces no result. This is a real SQLite
+  statement/transaction failure injection, not an actual disk I/O fault and not
+  proof of every SQLite I/O error's behavior.
+
+Both traces share the known missing recovery mechanism; they are not two new
+independent defects. A supported fix direction is to reconstruct incomplete
+running obligations on startup and make post-claim failures eligible for live
+retry as well, retaining atomic result/completion persistence. No repair was
+requested or made.
+
+## Executed coverage
+
+Run `python3 tests/hunt_witness.py`. It exits nonzero on violated expected
+behavior and zero if all six fixed cases hold. Current exit: **1**.
+
+| Case | Observed result |
+| --- | --- |
+| Abrupt exit after acceptance, before work | PASS: pending job completes after restart |
+| Abrupt exit after running commit | FAIL: accepted job abandoned |
+| Abrupt exit after completion | PASS: result survives, no further work |
+| Same ID and payload retried before/after completion | PASS: result retained, completed work not repeated |
+| Failed submission statement, then retry | PASS: exception instead of acceptance, no inserted job, retry completes |
+| Failed result transaction, then live retry/restart | FAIL: rollback is atomic but job remains abandoned |
+
+These are fixed concrete tests of real Queue entry points, not a generated or
+exhaustive search. Temporary databases and triggers are isolated and cleaned;
+the crash test uses a real process exit without connection cleanup. Unexpected
+execution errors also produce nonzero status. The saved command was rerun after
+the final witness edit. The environment lacks the `python` alias; use `python3`.
+
+## Additional findings and remaining gaps
+
+No additional distinct implementation defect was established within this
+bounded surface. In particular, conflicting reuse of a job ID is not enough
+to prove a new acceptance defect: code reading shows `INSERT OR IGNORE` returns
+accepted while retaining the original payload, and the contract says an ID has
+one payload throughout its lifetime. It does not specify rejection or response
+semantics for a conflicting payload. Treat any desired conflict error as a
+contract question, not an established violation; that input was not executed
+as part of this witness.
+
+The export recovery claim remains falsified by the original counterexample.
+The new transaction-failure route also exposes missing live retry recovery.
+Passing cases do not resolve either route or establish a claim pass.
+
+Not exercised: real disk-full/OS I/O failures, process termination inside a
+SQLite commit, failure during schema creation/startup, response loss after
+acceptance, or every possible transaction boundary. Commit atomicity and
+durability rely on the supplied SQLite deployment contract; the implementation
+also visibly groups result insertion and done update in one transaction. No
+bounded test here proves unbounded liveness independently of the code argument.
+Multiple workers, overlapping requests, corruption, storage loss, adversarial
+inputs, and the separate note-storage surface were outside this hunt. These
+gaps neither narrow the governing guarantee nor accept new residual risk.
